  "slug": "optimal-transport-flow-matching-ea35",
  "summary": "Matched-protocol ADMET benchmarks now show model, acquisition and tuning gains are endpoint-dependent rather than uniform.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread treats evaluation protocol, not architecture, as the experimental variable in molecular property prediction. A protocol-matched study of seven active-learning acquisition strategies and two uncertainty methods across eight ADMET regression endpoints asks when acquisition actually improves prediction. A matched-budget, scaffold-split comparison of GNNs, frozen ChemBERTa and MolCLR embeddings, and fingerprints across six TDC endpoints finds no model family dominant. Ark-ADMET extends this to tuning, reporting that endpoint-specific choice of fingerprints, descriptors, GIN/MiniMol embeddings and CatBoost hyperparameters matches or beats leaderboard scores on 7 of 21 endpoints. PeptSol applies benchmark construction to protected-peptide solubility, with 1388 curated measurements and chemistry-aware splits over frozen encoders. Adjacent single-cell and antibody immunogenicity studies apply the same auditing stance, benchmarking foundation models against simple baselines and showing a format lookup beats sequence predictors.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-04"
}
